// Excessive Failed Document Retrieval Attempts (T1526 — Cloud Service Discovery)
//
// The original rule filters GetDocument failures to errorCode == "AccessDenied".
// That is the wrong error. AWS documents GetDocument's InvalidDocument as "The SSM
// document doesn't exist or the document isn't available to the user" — it is what
// a principal guessing names receives, for names that do not exist AND for
// documents that exist but are not shared with it. Filtering to AccessDenied keeps
// the misconfigured-client case and discards the enumeration.
//
// Count DISTINCT NAMES, not attempts. Ten retries against one document is a broken
// script; ten distinct names is a principal mapping what exists.
//
// And the enumerator who HAS ssm:GetDocument produces no errors at all, so no
// error-based rule can see them. The second query below covers that case by
// counting distinct names read successfully.
//
// `contains`, not `has`: error codes and document names are punctuated
// (AccessDeniedException, AWS-RunShellScript, SSM-SessionManagerRunShell) and
// whole-term matching does not reach inside them.
//
// Dialect: Sentinel / Azure Log Analytics KQL. NOT CloudWatch Logs Insights.
AWSCloudTrail
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where EventSource == "ssm.amazonaws.com"
| where EventName in ("GetDocument", "DescribeDocument")
| extend Req = parse_json(RequestParameters)
| extend DocName = tostring(coalesce(Req.name, Req.Name, ""))
| where isnotempty(DocName)
| extend Outcome = case(
    isempty(ErrorCode),                          "success",
    ErrorCode contains "InvalidDocument",        "not-found-or-not-shared",
    ErrorCode contains "AccessDenied",           "iam-denied",
    "other-error")
| summarize
    Attempts        = count(),
    DistinctNames   = dcount(DocName),
    NotFound        = dcountif(DocName, Outcome == "not-found-or-not-shared"),
    IamDenied       = dcountif(DocName, Outcome == "iam-denied"),
    Succeeded       = dcountif(DocName, Outcome == "success"),
    FoundNames      = make_set_if(DocName, Outcome == "success", 25),
    ProbedNames     = make_set_if(DocName, Outcome != "success", 40),
    Errors          = make_set_if(ErrorCode, isnotempty(ErrorCode), 10),
    SourceIPs       = make_set(SourceIpAddress, 5),
    Agents          = make_set(UserAgent, 3),
    FirstSeen       = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen        = max(TimeGenerated)
    by UserIdentityArn, bin(TimeGenerated, 5m)
| where (NotFound + IamDenied) >= 10 or Succeeded >= 25
| extend HitRate = todouble(Succeeded) / todouble(DistinctNames)
| extend Verdict = case(
    (NotFound + IamDenied) >= 10 and Succeeded > 0,
        strcat("ENUMERATION THAT FOUND SOMETHING — read the documents in FoundNames now, P0"),
    NotFound >= 10,
        "DOCUMENT NAME ENUMERATION — names probed do not exist or are not shared, P1",
    IamDenied >= 10,
        "REPEATED IAM DENIAL ON GetDocument — a principal without the permission, P2",
    "BULK DOCUMENT READ BY A PERMITTED PRINCIPAL — no errors, error rules are blind here, P2")
| project FirstSeen, LastSeen, UserIdentityArn, Attempts, DistinctNames, NotFound,
          IamDenied, Succeeded, HitRate, FoundNames, ProbedNames, Errors,
          SourceIPs, Agents, Verdict
| order by DistinctNames desc
//
// HitRate is the shape signal. A deployment tool reading documents it owns is close
// to 1.0. A principal guessing names is close to 0.0 and its ProbedNames read like a
// wordlist rather than a naming scheme. A principal with permission enumerating
// everything is 1.0 with a very large DistinctNames — which is why the query keeps
// the Succeeded >= 25 arm.
//
// ============================================================================
// WHAT THE ENUMERATION WAS LOOKING FOR
// ============================================================================
// SSM documents are not secret by design, and people put things in them that are:
// bootstrap scripts with embedded tokens, hard-coded internal endpoints, the
// parameter names an application reads ({{ssm:/app/prod/db/password}}), and the
// automation assumeRole ARNs. A document that was read successfully has disclosed
// all of that. Fetch it and look:
//
//   aws ssm get-document --name <FoundName> --document-version '$LATEST' \
//     --query 'Content' --output text
//
// ============================================================================
// THE NEXT STEP, IF THEY FOUND ONE
// ============================================================================
// Enumeration is reconnaissance for execution. Pivot the same UserIdentityArn onto
// SendCommand, StartAutomationExecution, StartSession and CreateAssociation — all
// management events naming the document — and onto CreateDocument, which is the
// same actor authoring their own instead of borrowing yours. See
// ../../ssm.discovery.excessive-document-creation-detected/.
//
// ============================================================================
// ERROR CODES
// ============================================================================
// GetDocument: InvalidDocument ("The SSM document doesn't exist or the document
// isn't available to the user"), InvalidDocumentVersion, InternalServerError.
// IAM denials surface as AccessDenied, service-evaluated denials as
// AccessDeniedException — the contains match above catches both pairs. Confirm the
// exact strings against a real denied event in your own trail before narrowing.
//
// ============================================================================
// PREVENTION
// ============================================================================
// ssm:GetDocument and ssm:DescribeDocument support resource-level permissions on
// arn:aws:ssm:<region>:<account>:document/<name>. Scope reads to the documents a
// principal actually runs. Removing ssm:ListDocuments does NOT stop enumeration —
// it only forces it through GetDocument, where it becomes noisy and visible, which
// is the trade this rule set is built around.
